<section class="reference-section" id="jatayu-sacrifice"><div class="reference-section-heading"><h2>Jatayu’s Sacrifice · Courage and Dharma</h2><a href="/jatayu-sacrifice.html">Return to story</a></div><div class="reference-content"><p><strong>Valmiki Ramayana · English verse translation by Ralph T. H. Griffith.</strong> Original condensed prose retelling based on selected cantos in the owner-supplied edition. The scan is not distributed by this website.</p><ol><li><strong>Aranya Kanda 14.</strong><small>Supplied PDF pp. 1229–1234; printed pp. 245–247. Jatayu identifies himself as Dasharatha’s friend, offers to guard Sita and is embraced by Rama.</small></li><li><strong>Aranya Kanda 50.</strong><small>Supplied PDF pp. 1440–1444; printed pp. 287–288. Jatayu hears Sita, admonishes Ravana, acknowledges his age and resolves to prevent the abduction.</small></li><li><strong>Aranya Kanda 51.</strong><small>Supplied PDF pp. 1445–1451; printed pp. 288–290. Jatayu withstands Ravana’s arrows, destroys the bow and chariot, continues the defense and falls after Ravana attacks his wings.</small></li><li><strong>Aranya Kanda 68.</strong><small>Supplied PDF pp. 1539–1543; printed pp. 307–309. Rama and Lakshmana find Jatayu; he identifies Ravana, explains the broken chariot and tells how Sita was carried away.</small></li><li><strong>Aranya Kanda 69.</strong><small>Supplied PDF pp. 1544–1549; printed pp. 309–310. Jatayu gives the southern direction and Ravana’s lineage, dies, and receives funeral rites performed by Rama and Lakshmana.</small></li></ol><p>The canto numbering follows the supplied Griffith edition. Speeches, genealogical passages and graphic violence are condensed. “Courage is not measured only by the result” is an editorial reflection drawn from the episode’s sequence, not a translated verse.</p><p>All three images are newly AI-generated devotional interpretations. Figures, landscape, chariot and objects are imagined; the artwork is not presented as historical evidence.</p></div></section>
